# Batch 3 — Work area, pinning split, hotkey recorder, icon sizing, background apps

## 16. Taskbar doesn't reserve screen space / doesn't stay on top

Reported: when an app is maximized/fit to the full screen, the app extends
past the taskbar instead of stopping above it, and the taskbar doesn't stay
visually on top of it either. Two related problems:

- **Work area reservation isn't effective.** Per the architecture map,
  `TaskbarWindowController.swift` is meant to handle "work-area reservation"
  already — this needs debugging, since maximized apps are currently sizing
  themselves into the taskbar's space rather than stopping above it. Check
  whether the reserved-area API call (however it's currently implemented) is
  actually being applied/refreshed at the right time (e.g. on taskbar
  show/hide, on display change, on launch).
- **Taskbar panel isn't staying above other windows.** The taskbar's
  `NSPanel` window level should be high enough that even a maximized/fullscreen
  app never visually covers it. Verify the window level setting (e.g.
  `.statusBar` / `.floating` equivalent) and that it isn't being reset or
  dropped in some code path.

## 17. "Pin to Start" and "Pin to Taskbar" need to be separate

Currently pinning an app appears to affect both the Start menu and the
taskbar as one combined action. Right-click context menu on an app icon
should offer two independent options:
- **Pin to Start**
- **Pin to Taskbar**

An app should be pinnable to either, both, or neither, independently —
matching Windows 11. This likely means the underlying pinned-apps storage in
`Settings/TaskbarSettings.swift` needs to become two separate lists (or one
list with two boolean flags) instead of a single pinned-apps list currently
shared by both surfaces.

## 18. Hotkey recorder doesn't accept Option or Function keys

Follow-up to item #14 (custom Start menu hotkey). The recorder control
currently can't capture Option (⌥) as a modifier or Fn as the hotkey itself.
Check the key-capture logic — likely it's filtering/ignoring `NSEvent`
modifier flags for Option, and/or not listening for the Fn key event type at
all (Fn is reported differently than other modifiers on macOS — via
`NSEvent.ModifierFlags.function` — and may need explicit handling separate
from the standard Cmd/Ctrl/Opt/Shift modifier flags).

## 19. Icon size options look broken except "S"

Follow-up to item #11. Only the smallest icon size preset renders correctly;
other size presets (M, L, etc.) look visually wrong — likely padding,
corner-radius, or hover-highlight dimensions are hardcoded to the "S" values
elsewhere and not scaling proportionally with the rest of the icon. Check
`UI/AppIconView.swift` for any hardcoded frame/padding constants that should
instead be derived from the current icon-size setting.

## 20. Center alignment: Start button should move to center too

Follow-up to item #12. When center alignment is selected, the Start (grid)
icon is currently staying pinned to the far left instead of moving along with
the rest of the icon row as part of the centered group. The Start icon
should be treated as the first item *within* the centered icon row, not as a
fixed anchor separate from it.

## 21. Apps running in the background (window closed, process alive) don't reopen from taskbar

Reported case: Spotify's red close button puts it into a backgrounded state
(process still running, no window — common for menu-bar-style apps), and
clicking its taskbar icon afterward does nothing. Only apps that were fully
quit reopen correctly when clicked.

Root cause is likely in whatever click-handling logic decides how to respond
to an icon tap: if it's only prepared for "app not running → launch" and
"app running with a window → raise that window," it has no path for "app
running with zero windows → bring the app to the front / ask it to open a new
window." Fix: when the target app is running but has no windows, the click
handler should call `NSRunningApplication.activate(...)` on it (rather than
trying to find/raise a window that doesn't exist) so the app itself decides
what to show (e.g. Spotify would reopen its main window via its own logic).



